* START-HISTORY:
* 17 Oct 26 dm  Written for S.64 part 2 (the edit gates).  !WRITELIST is not $internal, so the write of the $savedlists pointer
*               to the VOC, which it did itself the first time a list was saved in an account that had none, was refused (12410) to
*               a session without voc.edit although all it was doing was saving a list.  SAVE.LIST and SAVE.STACK make the same
*               pointer from $internal programs.  This holds the pointer's creation and nothing else; the write of the list
*               stays in !WRITELIST, which is not $internal and so is still held to the gates for the file it writes to.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    call !mksavedlists(err)
*
* Makes the saved-lists file $svlists and the VOC entry $savedlists that names it, as SAVE.LIST does, when the VOC has no
* $savedlists entry.  ERR is 0 when the entry exists afterwards (made here, or already there), else the error number of the
* CREATE.FILE that failed.
*
* THIS PROGRAM IS $internal, WHICH THE KERNEL'S EDIT TEST LETS THROUGH, AND ANY PROGRAM CAN CALL IT BY NAME.  So it takes no
* name and no path from its caller, writes one fixed record, and does nothing at all when $savedlists already exists: it can
* never replace an entry the account has, which is what a change to the VOC would be.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine mksavedlists(err)
$catalog !mksavedlists

   err = 0

   * Hold the id, and look again: the caller found no entry, but another process may have made it since, and an entry that
   * is there is left exactly as it is.

   recordlocku @voc, '$savedlists'
   read s from @voc, '$savedlists' then
      release @voc, '$savedlists'
      return
   end

   create.file '$svlists' dynamic on error
      err = status()
      release @voc, '$savedlists'
      return
   end

   write 'F' : @fm : '$svlists' to @voc, '$savedlists'     ;* Writing the record releases the id

   return
end

* END-CODE
